00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.160
So I'll briefly talk about&nbsp;
what I think are some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:59.640
advantages that neural networks&nbsp;
can offer for physical modeling.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.400
I think these can be a powerful&nbsp;
tool to assist us in this task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:09.200
while also keeping in mind some of the&nbsp;
limitations, or some of the things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:16.240
we have to just know a priori or account a&nbsp;
priori when we're developing these methods.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:22.960
So the first point, and one of the big advantages&nbsp;
here is after you train a neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.160
when you actually go and test it or do&nbsp;
inference on new problems. This is quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:31.280
cheap because you've amortized&nbsp;
the computation during training.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.920
And neural networks, as we as we've seen&nbsp;
time and time and again, they're really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:40.960
really good at approximating high dimensional&nbsp;
functions, and this starts to become really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.720
nice when we're trying to solve these scientific&nbsp;
problems that are very high dimensional problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:52.080
and may have complicated solutions or&nbsp;
ways to get to complicated solutions

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.920
at the same time. I think there's&nbsp;
also some things to keep in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.120
particularly with neural networks in&nbsp;
the context of scientific computing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:06.160
So how do they actually compare to these current&nbsp;
mechanistic models or even experiments in a lab?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.760
So there's always going to be this cost&nbsp;
of training, this cost of data generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:16.600
and because we have these nice models we've&nbsp;
developed for many, many decades that came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:21.440
before neural networks, I think it's important&nbsp;
to keep in mind these models themselves at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:28.120
same time as we're developing new methods that are&nbsp;
going to bring in this idea of machine learning.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:32.760
And the other point here, another thing&nbsp;
to keep in mind is the accuracy point. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.840
it's not very useful if a neural network&nbsp;
is really fast, but it converges quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:41.080
to the wrong answers. Since this is again,&nbsp;
something really important for scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:45.160
modeling that we want something that&nbsp;
is going to be in a decent ballpark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:50.400
that's going to be at least a good initial&nbsp;
guess to getting us to the right solutions.

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:55.680
And then finally, I think this other point,&nbsp;
again, important for scientific modeling is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.200
this idea of reliability and methods.&nbsp;
So a lot of the numerical methods that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:05.680
have been developed over the decades have&nbsp;
really very strict reliability criteria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.720
or have been really stress tested to ensure some&nbsp;
level of reliability. And so can we actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:18.480
keep that in mind when we're bringing in this&nbsp;
component of machine learning into these problems?

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:24.040
And so I'll start with a really example, common&nbsp;
scenario. And this is really going to be the basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:29.080
of the rest of this talk, as I describe some of&nbsp;
the methods that we've been working on now that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:35.800
go beyond some of these common scenarios or some&nbsp;
of these more toy examples. And in this example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:41.320
this is some type of damping pendulum, so&nbsp;
you might have some training data in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:46.400
early part of this trajectory, and your&nbsp;
goal is to predict the later dynamics of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:53.720
this system. And this could be governed by this&nbsp;
very simple PDE. So this is a really toy example.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:58.360
So if you train a neural network on predicting&nbsp;
only the points in your training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.360 --> 00:08:02.800
so you train it by minimizing this loss&nbsp;
function to match those training data points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:07.400
you can see that it doesn't do very well when&nbsp;
it's trying to predict the later time steps, even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:14.200
though it gets the early time steps that it's seen&nbsp;
before, quite well, which is what we would expect.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:20.080
But one approach here that I think many of&nbsp;
you will be very, very familiar with is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:26.080
idea that you could add that underlying ODE, or&nbsp;
possibly an underlying PDE as a soft constraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:31.280
to the neural network loss function, and you&nbsp;
essentially optimize over this so you have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:36.200
training data. But then you, since you have some&nbsp;
form of the differential equation, this could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:42.000
some type of conservation law, you add that to&nbsp;
the loss function, and you essentially enforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:46.800
that over all of the later points in the domain.&nbsp;
And when you train with both of these objectives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.560
you can see that in this case, it seems like it's&nbsp;
actually fitting the later dynamics, because you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:58.000
were giving some implicit information, some self&nbsp;
supervisor auxiliary information about the later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.760
time steps. And this is very colloquially known as&nbsp;
this physics informed neural network loss, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:12.000
you have these physical laws that are enforced&nbsp;
over points in the domain, so some discretization.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.280
So the one thing about this type of approach,&nbsp;
on one hand, it's potentially a way to deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:22.560
with limited data, or if you have some type&nbsp;
of conservation laws you'd like to enforce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.840
it's potentially one way to try to add in that&nbsp;
type of information. But I think in as maybe many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.960
of you have dealt with if you've trained some&nbsp;
of these models, adding the physical laws this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:39.200
way doesn't necessarily guarantee the convergence&nbsp;
or correct solution or enforcement of those laws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.400
and often it takes a lot more beyond that&nbsp;
to actually get some type of convergence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:52.000
property. And this is just a simple example of a&nbsp;
convection problem where you have just this 1d PD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.200
as you increase this wave speed parameter,&nbsp;
you can see that this type of training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.120
doesn't converge very well to the later&nbsp;
time steps. So the left hand side is the.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:02.640
Actual solution in the right hand side is what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:08.600
would happen when you train the model&nbsp;
and then predict with this solution.

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:14.280
So that brings, yeah, there is a&nbsp;
question on Slide seven. Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.280 --> 00:10:19.760
want to answer that first? Oh, sure,&nbsp;
I actually can't see this screen.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.040
So So what

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:28.760
Pearson asked? What would happen if you&nbsp;
only gave the first data point but then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:33.560
enforce the PDE constraint? Would&nbsp;
you solve the PDE? Yeah. So if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:39.320
only gave this initial condition up here&nbsp;
and then predicted the rest of the PDE,

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:44.760
yeah, yeah. I think it really depends on the&nbsp;
problem setting. I think in a very simple problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:48.960
setting like this one, I think that would work&nbsp;
fine if you just gave the initial condition and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:54.280
then just optimized over the rest of the PD, so&nbsp;
you might not even need those training data points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.280 --> 00:11:01.840
in the first place. But this setting doesn't do&nbsp;
super well when you go to harder problem settings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:09.320
if you just do this kind of ad hoc approach. And&nbsp;
so one example here is that in this case, only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:13.200
the training was done by only giving the initial&nbsp;
condition and the boundary conditions and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.960
predicting a solution over the rest of the domain.&nbsp;
And as you can see, in this case, especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:24.720
when you have very sharp features in your&nbsp;
solution, this becomes a lot harder to capture.

