00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:39.600
or let's move on. Great. Then feel free&nbsp;
to ask questions throughout the talk.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:46.320
So that brings me to one of the main idea&nbsp;
of this talk, which is really this idea of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.800
how do we resolve some of these underlying&nbsp;
issues when we try to solve PDS with machine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:58.400
learning? And there's a few key points&nbsp;
here, I think, to keep in mind. So one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:05.480
of those points is that this idea of physical&nbsp;
consistency or having an accurate solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:10.640
this is especially important at inference.&nbsp;
So at test time, when we actually want to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:16.640
use the neural network for some type of task or&nbsp;
deploy it for some type of task, and the current&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:22.720
approaches that I've mentioned, whether it's data&nbsp;
driven or you add this self supervised PDE loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:28.000
there's no guarantee of those physical laws&nbsp;
being obeyed at test time in particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:34.440
and so that's potentially posing a problem for&nbsp;
how you would actually reliably deploy this.

00:12:34.440 --> 00:12:42.680
The other point is that these so far are all grid&nbsp;
based methods, so this means that your solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:48.720
is over some type of discretized domain, and that&nbsp;
means that you could have some type of error that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:54.480
comes up because there's some inherent error in&nbsp;
that discretization. And it can also be expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:13:00.280
because, especially if you're training with some&nbsp;
type of PDE loss, it requires differentiating back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:03.480
through that if you're going to take derivatives,&nbsp;
if you have higher order derivatives than your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:10.600
loss and if your architecture is more complicated,&nbsp;
that can start to get expensive to train.

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:16.560
And there's also some other points here. So&nbsp;
sometimes, if you have some type of physical law,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:21.960
and it's only approximately enforced, so the soft&nbsp;
constraint added to the loss function, this can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:27.520
sometimes be hard to optimize over and tricky to&nbsp;
actually get to converge. And again, you have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:33.600
first issue that this type of approach doesn't&nbsp;
guarantee any convergence at test time. And all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:39.200
these issues all become particularly noticeable in&nbsp;
limited data settings when you don't have a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.200 --> 00:13:47.920
data, each of these issues really comes up again&nbsp;
and again and becomes a bigger and bigger problem.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:53.440
So the general theme of this talk is really going&nbsp;
to be about this idea of numerical methods that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:58.680
have been really successful historically at&nbsp;
modeling complex systems and machine learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.680 --> 00:14:04.920
that's been very fast and successful at these&nbsp;
prediction tasks in a lot of different areas.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:10.600
How do we take both of these and get the best&nbsp;
of both worlds when we develop the next set of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.600 --> 00:14:16.680
methods for scientific modeling problems? And both&nbsp;
of these have their limitations too. So numerical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:23.680
methods can be quite slow. Machine learning can&nbsp;
be hard to get accurate or hard to generalize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:28.480
to new problems. And so we're hoping that by&nbsp;
taking the best of both worlds, we can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:34.880
limit the actual limitations of both of these&nbsp;
approaches, and by combining both, the goal is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:41.440
perhaps we can get better accuracy with less data,&nbsp;
as well as more robust methods downstream. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:45.520
the approaches that I'll talk about are some&nbsp;
of the things that we've been thinking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:53.720
and working on for exploring this intersection&nbsp;
between numerical methods and neural networks.

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:57.720
So the in for most of this talk, the&nbsp;
problem setting that I'll talk about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:59.940
is solving PDS that represent.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:04.960
And some type of complex system. So in this&nbsp;
case, this is an example of navier stokes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:09.280
equations. You might have some initial&nbsp;
conditions, like an initial vorticity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:15.320
and your goal is to predict the spatiotemporal&nbsp;
behavior of the system over time. And in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:20.680
cases, because these are spatiotemporal&nbsp;
phenomena or fluid problems that we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:24.240
a little bit about. We can sometimes&nbsp;
know that this might have some type&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.240 --> 00:15:28.960
of conservation of momentum, it might have&nbsp;
some type of conservation of mass, and so on.

00:15:32.440 --> 00:15:39.560
So the first issue I talked about was this idea&nbsp;
that physical laws are not imposed at test time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:44.640
and this can be when it matters most to have&nbsp;
that physical consistency. And this can also be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:48.320
sometimes be useful to enforce&nbsp;
these laws at training time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:52.440
because that ends up actually improving&nbsp;
convergence. This ends up helping a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:57.920
especially in limited data settings. And so the&nbsp;
first solution I'll talk about is implementing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:06.440
PDE constrained optimization as a layer in a&nbsp;
neural network to tackle some of these issues.

00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:11.040
So before the neural network setup looked&nbsp;
something like this, where you have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:16.360
spatial temporal points in your domain, you&nbsp;
use a neural network to predict the solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.360 --> 00:16:22.800
and you could train to either matching the&nbsp;
solution loss over your domain. You could also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:28.960
add this differential equation loss for the PDE so&nbsp;
this soft constraint approach, and then to train a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.960 --> 00:16:33.440
neural network, we would back propagate through&nbsp;
this loss function with respect to the weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:39.960
of the neural network. And after we train the&nbsp;
neural network, we can go test it on new problems.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:46.800
As mentioned before, there are some potential&nbsp;
issues with this approach, and so the thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:53.080
that we try to do instead is actually look at,&nbsp;
can we have more control over these physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:59.120
laws and enforcing these physical laws? And&nbsp;
to do so, we develop this framework to add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:04.120
a PD constraint optimization layer to a&nbsp;
neural network architecture. So this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:11.000
high level overview, but I'll go into some of the&nbsp;
important pieces and details. And in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:19.560
this PDE constrained optimization layer can be&nbsp;
added on top of any neural network architecture.

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:24.760
So the problem setting here is essentially&nbsp;
differential operator learning, where we're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:31.960
to find some operator that maps some parameters&nbsp;
here to some solutions u, such that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.960 --> 00:17:35.960
conservation law or that operator is enforced,&nbsp;
and we know that it's going to be equal to

00:17:35.960 --> 00:17:41.000
zero. So what we do here is we&nbsp;
take a neural network. So this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:44.240
is a neural network F, parametrized by some theta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:49.480
and we want to map the neural network the&nbsp;
parameters of your physical system to B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:56.520
which is a set of n basis functions. So our neural&nbsp;
network is trying to do this parameter mapping,

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:01.920
and we formulate this instead as we want to&nbsp;
find some omega such that when you take this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.920 --> 00:18:07.200
product of those basis functions with respect&nbsp;
to omega, this is going to satisfy whatever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.200 --> 00:18:16.240
your conservation law is. So this equivalency is&nbsp;
trying to make an approximation to your solution u

00:18:16.240 --> 00:18:21.560
and so what we do is we solve for this&nbsp;
omega value using some type of iterative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.560 --> 00:18:27.360
nonlinear least squares solver. So this could be&nbsp;
LM, it could be gauss Newton, or whatever your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:33.280
favorite solver is. And this is done by sampling&nbsp;
a set of spatiotemporal points on your domain.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:38.720
And after we solve this problem, the final&nbsp;
solution operator prediction is reconstructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:45.960
together with having solved for a reasonable value&nbsp;
of omega with your numerical solver, and so you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:50.720
then having some approximation for the solution&nbsp;
using this approach instead. So this is giving you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:56.040
a bit more precise control over actually enforcing&nbsp;
this conservation law, because you're explicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.040 --> 00:19:04.000
using a solver to try to actually approximate&nbsp;
that PDE constrained optimization problem.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:08.720
So when you look at this setting, I went through&nbsp;
essentially what's a forward pass of the neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:14.480
network. But the issue is that when you run that&nbsp;
solver, so you project your outputs of the neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:21.760
network onto some solver, and that's the solver&nbsp;
that you're running to now actually learn the PDEs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:27.520
more precisely. The issue is that when you take&nbsp;
all of these steps of your solver, you still have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:32.600
to train the neural network somehow. And doing&nbsp;
standard backpropagation, where you differentiate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:37.640
back through all of those steps, is really&nbsp;
expensive. It's actually usually impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:43.640
because you run out of memory very quickly. And&nbsp;
so to get around this issue, what we did instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:50.480
is some so we looked at this problem, and we see&nbsp;
that what we want to do is the solver is run after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:55.440
the neural network to come to a solution. And&nbsp;
if we want to actually train the neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.440 --> 00:20:00.240
we need to differentiate back, and we need&nbsp;
to do some type of gradient descent on theta.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:03.240
Our neural network parameters to&nbsp;
learn what the correct solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:08.640
is. And so we use implicit function&nbsp;
theorem and implicit differentiation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:13.280
which I think is a really powerful tool when&nbsp;
you're doing anything with combining numerical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:19.360
methods and neural networks. And what that does&nbsp;
is you can formulate this differentiation problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:24.480
in an implicit way. So what that means is that&nbsp;
you don't need to differentiate back through all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:24.480 --> 00:20:28.800
the steps of your solver. So you don't have to&nbsp;
back propagate through the whole computational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:34.120
graph that got you to that final point. You can&nbsp;
just differentiate the last step and bypass all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.120 --> 00:20:40.080
of those intermediate steps. And a lot of&nbsp;
the details, you can check out the paper for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:45.200
a lot of the details on the full framework&nbsp;
and the exact problem that you're solving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:51.960
but this is essentially the trick that makes it&nbsp;
possible to combine these two things together.

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:56.560
So coming back to this and the full&nbsp;
problem setup. So now we have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:56.560 --> 00:21:01.360
neural network architecture we project onto&nbsp;
this PDE constrained optimization layer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:01.360 --> 00:21:06.360
which solves the optimization problem, more&nbsp;
precisely, that represents our conservation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:11.600
laws. We use implicit differentiation to train&nbsp;
the model. So what it means is that the forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.600 --> 00:21:16.000
pass of the neural network is decoupled from&nbsp;
the backward pass of the neural network, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:21.320
that allows us to train this whole thing, end to&nbsp;
end, with both the neural network and the solver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:25.160
and this means that the neural network is&nbsp;
able to get direct feedback from the solver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:30.560
so the weights can update based on this exact&nbsp;
solve that happens in the PDE constrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:36.040
optimization layer. And then at test time, we&nbsp;
can go ahead and run this neural network on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:41.880
new problem. And because the forward pass of the&nbsp;
neural network still goes through this layer here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:48.600
it means that those constraints are more&nbsp;
precisely enforced at test time as well.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:54.800
So here's a very simple example of when we first&nbsp;
tried this out. So this was for a Darcy flow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.800 --> 00:22:01.080
problem, very simple PDE, where you have some PDE&nbsp;
parameters, in this case, diffusion coefficients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.080 --> 00:22:07.600
and you want to learn what the PDE solution is.&nbsp;
And so we ran this with a baseline of we just add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:13.280
the conservation laws to the loss function. This&nbsp;
soft constraint approach, which does, does okay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.280 --> 00:22:20.840
but doesn't really converge that well, and it&nbsp;
sort of plateaus at this point. And our new model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:25.040
where we implement this new layer, which&nbsp;
is acts more like a hard constraint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:30.080
and you can see that it starts at a much&nbsp;
lower point and then continues to go down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:34.200
And this is on some unseen test set, so&nbsp;
we just track how well it's doing during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:40.600
training on this test set. So it does tend to&nbsp;
converge more quickly for a problem like this.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:46.920
But that being said, I mentioned that during&nbsp;
training, you're going through this new layer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.920 --> 00:22:50.760
and differentiating it, but also during test&nbsp;
time, you also have to go through that new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:50.760 --> 00:22:58.200
layer. And what that means is that as you enforce&nbsp;
this layer and this whole setup on more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:04.120
points which can happen or is quite necessary&nbsp;
when your problem gets quite hard, that also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.120 --> 00:23:10.120
means that your computational expense and memory&nbsp;
cost increases. So this is an example of solving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:16.800
the 2d navier stokes equations. So as we increase&nbsp;
the number of sampled points, it's likely that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:23.280
model does better, because it's able to solve&nbsp;
for a larger domain and force whatever physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:29.480
laws over a larger domain. But the issue is that&nbsp;
as you increase these points, both your training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.480 --> 00:23:34.720
and your inference time get larger and larger.&nbsp;
So there's this limitation of the computational&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:34.720 --> 00:23:40.320
trade off. And if you sample more points in this&nbsp;
setup, you often need to have a lower batch size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:44.000
because of the memory costs, and that ends up&nbsp;
actually hurting training stability. So for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.000 --> 00:23:52.200
some of these much harder problems, it becomes&nbsp;
very difficult to solve with this current setup.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:58.440
So to get around that setting, we used a mixture&nbsp;
of experts approach to actually scale this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.960 --> 00:24:04.920
full architecture, and mixture of experts&nbsp;
really has made it possible to scale a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:11.080
of very large models in many other fields&nbsp;
of machine learning, like NLP and vision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:11.080 --> 00:24:15.000
and it's this nice way of actually increasing&nbsp;
the capacity of the neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:20.080
but still balancing the computational expense&nbsp;
where now you split the computation over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:25.880
bunch of different experts that solve&nbsp;
these different sub domain problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:30.920
and so we wanted to see if we could actually&nbsp;
bring this idea of mixture of experts to our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.920 --> 00:24:37.560
physical modeling problem and actually be&nbsp;
able to scale it to much harder systems.

00:24:37.560 --> 00:24:44.080
So we frame this PDA constrained optimization&nbsp;
problem in our neural network as a mixture of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:48.440
experts instead. And the main difference&nbsp;
is, before we had this neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.440 --> 00:24:54.920
this f of theta, and we would go ahead and go&nbsp;
straight to the end here and run our solver,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:59.920
do our prediction, and this time,&nbsp;
we actually split our our domain.

00:24:59.920 --> 00:25:06.720
Into a bunch of different subdomains and use an&nbsp;
expert to solve the problem along each subdomain.

00:25:06.720 --> 00:25:11.080
So what that fully looks like is you have&nbsp;
some type of neural network. Here. We use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:17.920
a neural operator for this type of problem.&nbsp;
It doesn't matter which architecture you use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.920 --> 00:25:22.600
though, it could be your favorite one. And&nbsp;
this whole part here is our PE constrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.600 --> 00:25:28.480
optimization layer. And to scale it up, we&nbsp;
do this domain decomposition where we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:28.480 --> 00:25:33.880
a bunch of different subdomains, and we enforce&nbsp;
this constrained optimization problem over each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:38.880
subdomain. So these are the hard constraints&nbsp;
that are enforced over these subdomains by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:43.960
the different experts. And so this makes those&nbsp;
constraints localized, and it's split across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.960 --> 00:25:50.720
multiple experts here, so it's not just&nbsp;
one single one, but many different ones.

00:25:50.720 --> 00:25:53.960
And the advantage of this is that now&nbsp;
we've actually decomposed our domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:57.920
and we can actually solve this problem&nbsp;
in parallel. So when we put it all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.920 --> 00:26:03.120
together, if we have some PDE domain, I've&nbsp;
kept it very simple here, just a 2d problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:10.120
but we also do this on 3d and 40 settings. You&nbsp;
can give this PD domain to the neural network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:10.120 --> 00:26:15.720
and it'll go through this whole process of&nbsp;
you have these learnable basis functions here.

00:26:15.720 --> 00:26:21.120
You evaluate and partition them over your&nbsp;
sub domains. You have this mixture of expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.120 --> 00:26:25.760
setting, where each expert locally solves the&nbsp;
problem. And so it looks something like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:33.040
where within each expert you're still doing that&nbsp;
same task of finding a set of omega that satisfies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:38.320
this product here and actually satisfies the&nbsp;
PDE of interest. So it's the local constraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.320 --> 00:26:44.840
over each subdomain. And then you can go ahead and&nbsp;
reassemble your full predicted solution together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:44.840 --> 00:26:50.520
and then putting it all together, you go ahead&nbsp;
and differentiate your output, and then you use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.520 --> 00:26:55.800
implicit function theorem to actually train the&nbsp;
full model end to end. And when you do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:55.800 --> 00:27:02.520
that means that each expert is both being used in&nbsp;
the forward pass, but also in the backwards pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:08.160
through this implicit differentiation technique.&nbsp;
So this allows you to actually scale the model and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:08.160 --> 00:27:15.040
train it over a lot of different problems, because&nbsp;
everything can also be done in parallel now.

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:19.520
So as an example of what that looks like,&nbsp;
this is, this is a problem, diffusion sorption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:19.520 --> 00:27:26.000
problem. It's a tricky problem, actually, because&nbsp;
it has a singularity at a certain point here, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:31.400
this is what the numerical solver solution would&nbsp;
look like. And if you're using a soft constraint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.400 --> 00:27:36.400
approach, because of some of those difficulties,&nbsp;
you can see here that in a lot of cases, it's not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:36.400 --> 00:27:43.640
really able to converge to any solution. So when&nbsp;
we use our initial approach where we just have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:48.720
a hard constraint, we start to do a little bit&nbsp;
better. But I think it's limited to some extent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:52.960
because you need to sample a lot of points for&nbsp;
this problem, and because we can't fit all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:58.440
those points, we often have to use a lower batch&nbsp;
size, so it actually hurts our training stability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:03.920
But when we go ahead and scale this with this new&nbsp;
mixture of experts approach, we get a lot closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:10.400
to what the true solution should be, so it starts&nbsp;
to match the numerical solution pretty well.

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:16.280
Another advantage is this idea of lacking&nbsp;
training stability. So when we actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:23.160
train with our regular layer, with no mixture&nbsp;
of experts, it's not doing super well because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:23.160 --> 00:28:28.480
of the fact that during training it's unstable.&nbsp;
And it's not just because of this limitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:33.240
that it can't sample enough points&nbsp;
because of computational expense,

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:41.240
but when you actually then train with this&nbsp;
Moe setting, this is another sample that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:46.360
pulled out of the system. So we had a test set&nbsp;
of a few 100 samples. In this case, when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:46.360 --> 00:28:51.840
actually train with that Moe setting, you're&nbsp;
a lot more stable and predictable when you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.840 --> 00:29:00.160
doing the training. So it's converging slowly&nbsp;
to the right solution in a more stable way. And

00:29:00.160 --> 00:29:06.160
then when we come back to this plot where we saw&nbsp;
that one of the limitations before was that as you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:06.160 --> 00:29:11.040
increase the number of points, your training&nbsp;
and inference time gets slower and slower. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:16.480
this case, now, when you increase the number of&nbsp;
points, this scale sub linearly. So this is a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:20.800
more efficient. I think this is quite important,&nbsp;
because as we get to the whole point of neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:27.520
networks is to be faster than numerical solvers,&nbsp;
and at inference time. In particular, if running a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:27.520 --> 00:29:32.520
neural network is slower than running a numerical&nbsp;
solver, there's not really much of an advantage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:38.400
to the neural network approach. So getting this&nbsp;
down to some level of computational efficiency and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:38.400 --> 00:29:46.120
speed, I think, is very important for actually&nbsp;
the whole purpose of using neural networks.

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:51.600
So we'll come back to some of these&nbsp;
underlying issues that we looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.600 --> 00:29:57.440
earlier about resolving solving PDS with&nbsp;
machine learning. And the second issue I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:57.440 --> 00:30:00.100
had mentioned was this idea that grid based meth.

00:30:00.100 --> 00:30:06.080
Methods introduce errors. So when you solve for a&nbsp;
solution over a discretized domain, that's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:06.080 --> 00:30:12.840
to inherently introduce some errors. And so one&nbsp;
solution that we thought of when we looked at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:12.840 --> 00:30:19.120
problem was, again, an idea of taking spectral&nbsp;
methods, which have been really successful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:19.120 --> 00:30:24.400
used quite well in applied mathematics and&nbsp;
scientific computing, and seeing if we could take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.400 --> 00:30:31.080
the advantages of spectral methods and combine&nbsp;
that with the advantages of neural networks.

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:35.320
So coming back to this setting of this&nbsp;
physics and form neural network loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:41.960
so this would be like a self supervised setting&nbsp;
for solving PDS. So when you optimize over this PD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:41.960 --> 00:30:47.200
residual in the loss function, there's this first&nbsp;
point that you're optimizing over physical points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:52.840
in your domain. So those grid based methods&nbsp;
introduce errors. It can also require a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:52.840 --> 00:30:58.160
number of sampled points. So if your problem is&nbsp;
hard, you may need a lot of points to optimize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:58.160 --> 00:31:06.080
over. And so this approach also just has issues&nbsp;
with scaling to large grid sizes. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:11.720
if you're looking at this setting where you're&nbsp;
using a neural operator and then optimizing over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.720 --> 00:31:17.200
it with some type of PDE loss, this can be&nbsp;
expensive to differentiate back through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:17.200 --> 00:31:23.280
architecture, especially for more complicated&nbsp;
architectures like the Fourier neural operator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:26.960
it means that some of the scaling&nbsp;
actually doesn't do super well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:26.960 --> 00:31:32.880
especially if you have higher order&nbsp;
derivatives that you're trying to enforce.

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:39.560
So spectral methods have been very powerful for&nbsp;
solving PDEs. So these basis function expansions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:45.040
you could use Chebyshev polynomials. As one&nbsp;
example, you could use la gendre polynomials.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:45.040 --> 00:31:50.560
They can be quite powerful and efficient, and&nbsp;
when you're learning PDS in in this spectral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:50.560 --> 00:31:57.240
representation, you can really do accurate&nbsp;
approximations of differentiation and integration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:04.640
or you can really do accurate approximations of&nbsp;
algebraic operations going out to a certain when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.640 --> 00:32:09.560
you go out to a certain number of terms when&nbsp;
you do that basis expansion. And so I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:09.560 --> 00:32:15.080
these have a lot of potential to also think about&nbsp;
how they can be integrated with neural networks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:15.080 --> 00:32:21.880
because there are so many advantages of these&nbsp;
methods in the scientific computing setting.

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:27.440
And so to take the ideas from spectral&nbsp;
methods and neural networks and really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:27.440 --> 00:32:34.200
get the best of both worlds, we came up with&nbsp;
this neural spectral methods approach where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:34.200 --> 00:32:39.640
we have in our base architecture some type of&nbsp;
spectral based neural operator. It's not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:39.640 --> 00:32:45.640
limited to Fourier basis. So you can actually use&nbsp;
Chebyshev polynomials or Legendre polynomials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:49.920
so really any spectral basis function, and we&nbsp;
actually find that for a lot of the problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:49.920 --> 00:32:55.080
we look at, using Chebyshev basis actually&nbsp;
works a lot better than a Fourier basis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.080 --> 00:32:59.120
particularly in cases where you don't&nbsp;
have periodic boundary conditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:59.120 --> 00:33:06.120
as what you would expect for when you see how&nbsp;
where they're used in scientific computing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:11.480
And we also have this spectral loss function,&nbsp;
and I'll go through each of these. And this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:11.480 --> 00:33:16.640
in contrast this idea of physical space learning&nbsp;
that had some of the limitations mentioned before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:22.400
that it could be harder to learn, and&nbsp;
you have these grid size limitations.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:27.560
So we start, in this case, in our base&nbsp;
architecture by having we have parameters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:27.560 --> 00:33:31.360
to solutions. This is the same setting&nbsp;
that we want to learn. And we look at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:31.360 --> 00:33:36.720
spectral representation of our parameters.&nbsp;
We do these spectral transformations. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:36.720 --> 00:33:42.080
this is where we're deciding what basis&nbsp;
functions we want to use, in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:42.080 --> 00:33:48.960
Chebyshev polynomials tend to be the&nbsp;
ones that end up working quite well.

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:53.600
And then, for last point, we use&nbsp;
the spectral training procedure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:53.600 --> 00:33:58.040
where we can actually use parsevals&nbsp;
identity to come up with an equivalency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.040 --> 00:34:03.920
between some type of PDE loss in your physical&nbsp;
domain. And what that spectral representation is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:09.360
because you can have this equivalency through the&nbsp;
sum of the square of your spectral coefficients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:09.360 --> 00:34:14.320
and that essentially gives you a very good&nbsp;
approximation for what the PE residual of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:14.320 --> 00:34:20.840
problem is. And what that means is that you can&nbsp;
actually not just project to spectral space and do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:20.840 --> 00:34:26.560
all of your architecture transformations in this&nbsp;
part of the architecture. You can then train the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:26.560 --> 00:34:33.000
whole model fully in spectral space by using this&nbsp;
spectral loss function, and that allows you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:39.080
just do everything in the spectral domain, which&nbsp;
will have some advantages, as I'll talk about now.

00:34:39.080 --> 00:34:42.880
But just a quick note is that this&nbsp;
is in contrast to training in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:42.880 --> 00:34:47.920
physical domain. If you have some grid based&nbsp;
neural operator and you have this PDE loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:50.520
or this physics informed neural network loss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.520 --> 00:34:57.640
so this would be so the top part is the spectral&nbsp;
approach, in contrast to this physical approach.

00:34:58.280 --> 00:34:59.960
So here's one example.

00:34:59.960 --> 00:35:02.960
Where we looked at a reaction&nbsp;
diffusion system. So in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:07.840
we have, this is just a 2d problem. You&nbsp;
have some temporal dimension and some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:14.240
spatial dimension. And we ran a numerical&nbsp;
solver on this so you see that it behaves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:14.240 --> 00:35:21.160
very predictably. As you increase the resolution&nbsp;
of your domain, the error gets lower and lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:21.160 --> 00:35:25.320
and this error is relative to a very fine&nbsp;
discretization. So we generated a really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:31.480
fine solution, and then we sub sampled it to&nbsp;
represent all of these different grid points.

00:35:31.480 --> 00:35:38.640
So if you use this approach of a standard grid&nbsp;
based neural operator, and you add and then you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:44.840
add some type of PDE loss to that. So this would&nbsp;
be training only in physical space. You can see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:44.840 --> 00:35:51.280
that it doesn't quite match up super well, so&nbsp;
it's slower with a finer discretization. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.320
one thing is that you can see that it actually&nbsp;
overfits a little bit to the training grid that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:55.320 --> 00:36:00.560
it was trained on. So it actually sometimes&nbsp;
will do worse for higher training grids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:05.800
rather than decrease continuously, like this&nbsp;
standard numerical solver. So this would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:11.960
like the grid based approaches. And in contrast,&nbsp;
when you do this all in the spectral domain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:11.960 --> 00:36:16.840
so you use the approach I talked about, where&nbsp;
you have this neural network, you train it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.840 --> 00:36:23.560
with a spectral loss, you end up down here for&nbsp;
all of your grid sizes, because now the grid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:23.560 --> 00:36:28.840
no longer matters, because your computational&nbsp;
cost is constant regardless of the grid size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:28.840 --> 00:36:33.200
because you're naturally not expressing your&nbsp;
loss function in terms of the grid itself. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.200 --> 00:36:38.160
there's no sampling issue here, and you can see&nbsp;
that it maintains accuracy. So when you compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:44.680
it to the standard numerical solver, it's faster&nbsp;
for the same level of accuracy, which I think is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:44.680 --> 00:36:53.960
the comparison that we should be doing for all new&nbsp;
neural net work methods in this ml for PDE space.

00:36:53.960 --> 00:36:59.120
We also went and looked at different forms of the&nbsp;
Navier stokes equations. So this is an example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:04.080
where we're looking at unforced navier stokes&nbsp;
equations, where the top row is our numerical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:09.280
solver, the middle row is our method, and the&nbsp;
bottom row is when you train only in physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:09.280 --> 00:37:14.360
space. So this is actually an animation, but&nbsp;
we converge pretty quickly with this spectral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:14.360 --> 00:37:19.200
approach, whereas when you look at the bottom&nbsp;
row, there's a lot of features here that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:19.200 --> 00:37:25.840
don't converge to what the numerical solver looks&nbsp;
like, and these are somewhat hard to resolve.

00:37:25.840 --> 00:37:30.800
And this is another example where now we look at&nbsp;
force navier stokes equations. So we have this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:36.960
numerical solver we train only in physical&nbsp;
space, and this actually blows up over long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:36.960 --> 00:37:42.320
time scales. So the error gets really high as&nbsp;
you get further and further away in time, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:42.320 --> 00:37:48.800
when you do this approach with spectral methods,&nbsp;
it's a lot more well behaved. So over time, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:48.800 --> 00:37:55.840
total relative error ends up being a lot lower.&nbsp;
And I didn't plot the computational speed here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:55.840 --> 00:37:59.760
but that's also a very big advantage of&nbsp;
this approach, that it's a lot faster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.760 --> 00:38:08.120
both during training, but also when you&nbsp;
actually go and use this model during inference.

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:13.920
So the last thing I'll talk about is, so far&nbsp;
I've talked about this idea of inspiration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:19.800
for numerical methods and neural networks in&nbsp;
the context of solving PDS, so fluid dynamics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.800 --> 00:38:25.400
problems or other general types of PDs. But I&nbsp;
think these simulation in the loop ideas can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.400 --> 00:38:31.720
also be really relevant for other problems like&nbsp;
molecular systems or things at the atomistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:39.840
scale and running molecular dynamics. So this is&nbsp;
an approach here, where the way you train models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:39.840 --> 00:38:45.400
for molecular dynamics, a very common approach is&nbsp;
that you try to approximate the potential energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:45.400 --> 00:38:50.920
surface of these systems, and if you have a good&nbsp;
approximation for the energies and the forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:56.520
you can then run these simulations over&nbsp;
time by using Numerical Integrator. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.520 --> 00:39:01.200
hopefully you can run it for very long time&nbsp;
scales without it running into instabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:06.120
because then you can study the system better.&nbsp;
You can compute observables about the system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:06.120 --> 00:39:10.800
from that type of simulation. But there&nbsp;
are also some challenges here. So this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:16.200
an example of a neural network trained for&nbsp;
these molecular simulators. And we go ahead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.200 --> 00:39:23.120
and run this over in time, so we simulate&nbsp;
the system over, ideally long time steps.

00:39:23.120 --> 00:39:27.080
And initially it seems okay, but&nbsp;
as you simulate the system larger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.080 --> 00:39:32.560
longer and longer, you see that there's this&nbsp;
very clear, unphysical thing happening where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:39.160
there's this bond stretching that results&nbsp;
in a very unstable structure. And both,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:42.040
what that means is that so you&nbsp;
can't really trust the simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:46.960
because there are these instabilities coming up.&nbsp;
But any types of properties that you then want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:51.000
compute from that simulation are probably&nbsp;
going to be very inaccurate, because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.000 --> 00:39:57.320
simulation is just not stable enough to get to&nbsp;
the point of where you can do those computations.

00:39:59.000 --> 00:39:59.960
So coming back.

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:04.800
Back to the idea of simulation in the loop,&nbsp;
there's a lot of learning signal that you can get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:11.400
from the molecular simulation itself. So in this&nbsp;
case, this approach to slightly different problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.400 --> 00:40:16.360
setting in that you might have some atomistic&nbsp;
system, some molecular system, you give it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:16.360 --> 00:40:21.080
a neural network, and you predict the energies&nbsp;
and the forces of that system. And once you have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:21.080 --> 00:40:26.520
the forces, you can use numerical integration to&nbsp;
get to your next time step. And this is how you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.520 --> 00:40:33.680
would run the simulation over time. And as you run&nbsp;
this MD simulation over time, and keep running it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:33.680 --> 00:40:38.840
you can compute system observables about&nbsp;
that system, so something about that system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:38.840 --> 00:40:44.640
or multiple properties of that system. And&nbsp;
so typically, the common approach to train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:44.640 --> 00:40:49.760
these models is to train this neural network to&nbsp;
adjust the energies and forces. But in this case,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:49.760 --> 00:40:55.320
we wanted to actually train the neural network&nbsp;
to the simulation itself. So actually use all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.320 --> 00:41:00.000
this information of the simulation and build&nbsp;
that into the training procedure somehow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:04.480
because that would also then allow us to train&nbsp;
to more than just energies and forces. We could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:04.480 --> 00:41:09.920
actually train to observables of the system.&nbsp;
Maybe those observables come from experiment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:16.240
So this could be actually a way to bridge&nbsp;
some type of theory experiment gap as well.

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:21.680
And so the same idea here is when we go&nbsp;
and train to these observables over MD&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.680 --> 00:41:26.600
simulation. So we run the MD simulation, we&nbsp;
query the neural network, run the integrator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:26.600 --> 00:41:32.960
we compute system observables, and we take a loss&nbsp;
function on the observables. Now we come into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.960 --> 00:41:38.400
same issue that we had before, where you can't&nbsp;
differentiate back through all of the steps of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:38.400 --> 00:41:42.640
your MD simulation, because it means that you're&nbsp;
going to differentiate through all of the steps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:42.640 --> 00:41:47.960
of your Numerical Integrator. And so instead,&nbsp;
here we can do the same thing. We can train the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.960 --> 00:41:53.880
neural network with implicit differentiation. So&nbsp;
there's actually a way. It's a different approach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.880 --> 00:41:58.480
to formulate this implicit differentiation&nbsp;
problem than the previous approach I talked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:58.480 --> 00:42:02.680
about, because this is actually a stochastic&nbsp;
setting, so you're actually doing implicit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:02.680 --> 00:42:08.920
differentiation over a distribution. But&nbsp;
the key idea here is that you can formulate,

00:42:09.560 --> 00:42:14.000
taking the gradients this way. And the&nbsp;
key insight is really that you separate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:19.000
getting the out the neural network output&nbsp;
from the rest of the neural network itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:19.000 --> 00:42:24.560
so you don't have to back, propagate back through&nbsp;
all of the steps of the solver to get there.

00:42:24.560 --> 00:42:30.000
And so this ends up also being quite powerful in&nbsp;
the context of molecular dynamics. So this is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:37.160
example of a water system, and there's a local&nbsp;
instability that comes up. So the full simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:37.160 --> 00:42:42.120
in the loop method and the differentiation&nbsp;
technique, we call this Boltzmann estimator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:42.120 --> 00:42:47.400
because we formulate the implicit differentiation&nbsp;
problem based on the fact that we know that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:52.360
underlying system obeys a Boltzmann distribution.&nbsp;
And this is how you can actually formulate what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:52.360 --> 00:42:58.840
the differentiation task should be. And so&nbsp;
before training, when you use standard training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:58.840 --> 00:43:05.160
so the approach over here, where you just&nbsp;
train it with these energies and forces.

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:11.480
When you run this system over time, over all of&nbsp;
these picoseconds, all of the trajectories become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:11.480 --> 00:43:18.160
unstable relatively quickly, so often too quickly&nbsp;
before you can actually compute things that are of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:18.160 --> 00:43:22.400
interest, so observables of interest about the&nbsp;
system. But when you actually now train it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.400 --> 00:43:28.320
this simulation, in the loop style, after we do&nbsp;
our training procedure, we can actually simulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:34.800
this system for we went although we went up to one&nbsp;
nanosecond, but you can also go further than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.800 --> 00:43:41.680
then you can see that the system no longer&nbsp;
has the same instability problems as before.

00:43:41.680 --> 00:43:47.880
And the way that this nicely manifests is that&nbsp;
if we then want to go and actually compute things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:47.880 --> 00:43:53.520
from the system. So in this case, we don't train&nbsp;
it on anything about the diffusivity coefficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:57.840
so it doesn't see anything about this.&nbsp;
But after we train it with this procedure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:57.840 --> 00:44:03.720
and we go run it at test time, and we compute&nbsp;
the diffusivity coefficient of the system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.720 --> 00:44:09.080
we end up being a lot closer to what the true&nbsp;
coefficient is. So this black line here represents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:09.080 --> 00:44:15.040
the reference. And before training, all of these&nbsp;
before our training, the trajectories were not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:20.480
really getting any close to those reference&nbsp;
observables. And after our training, we're a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:20.480 --> 00:44:25.920
more in the ballpark or in the general range of&nbsp;
what that true diffusivity coefficient should be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:25.920 --> 00:44:32.000
And so I think this type of procedure is powerful&nbsp;
for both bridging this theory and experiment gap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:37.400
but also the fact that by doing this procedure,&nbsp;
you can improve both the stability and performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.400 --> 00:44:43.560
on new systems or new observables, and actually&nbsp;
have some level of hopefully more reliability in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:50.000
running those molecular simulations downstream.&nbsp;
And the very high level general idea of reading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:57.040
numerical simulations with neural networks is&nbsp;
still generally true here in this setting as well.

00:44:58.360 --> 00:45:00.160
So in summary, I think.

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:05.520
Think neural networks do have a lot of&nbsp;
potential for these physical modeling problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:05.520 --> 00:45:10.640
but at the same time, we really need to keep in&nbsp;
mind this idea that we need accurate and rigorous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:10.640 --> 00:45:16.040
methods that are going to be able to give us&nbsp;
reliable, physically consistent answers when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:21.480
we go run that method in a simulation, or&nbsp;
go run that neural network in a simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:21.480 --> 00:45:25.840
and this idea of also having trust during&nbsp;
deployment. So after we train a model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:25.840 --> 00:45:31.080
we would like to trust it when we actually go use&nbsp;
it for a lot of different downstream tasks. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:31.080 --> 00:45:37.320
so here I talked about a few different examples&nbsp;
of the best of both worlds. So taking numerical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:37.320 --> 00:45:42.400
methods and deep learning and putting these&nbsp;
together with this idea of differentiable solvers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:42.400 --> 00:45:48.360
so ways to actually come up with differentiation&nbsp;
techniques that let you train these two things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:53.760
together. So whether that's through this idea of&nbsp;
a PDE constrained optimization layer in the neural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:53.760 --> 00:46:00.840
network, or taking neural networks and combining&nbsp;
them with spectral methods, ideas, as well as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:06.600
fact that these simulation in the loop. Ideas also&nbsp;
extend to problems like molecular dynamics, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:06.600 --> 00:46:11.360
it's a bit of a different setting because you have&nbsp;
a stochastic setting, but you can still formulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:11.360 --> 00:46:18.680
the same differentiation approaches and train this&nbsp;
model to the output of a simulation itself, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:18.680 --> 00:46:23.600
ideally improve the simulation stability as well&nbsp;
as the accuracy of the properties that you compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:23.600 --> 00:46:30.960
from that simulation. And the papers with all the&nbsp;
code available for everything I talked about are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:30.960 --> 00:46:38.200
all down here, so you can definitely go check it&nbsp;
out and let us know if you have any questions.

